<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lviv</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lemberg, Lwów</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lviv is the largest city of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, founded in the mid-13th century by King Daniel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and named after his son Leo. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1349, was the capital of the Austrian crownland of Galicia as Lemberg from 1772 to 1918 and then belonged to Poland until 1939. Its historic centre is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lviv.jpg" alt="Lviv" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lestat (Jan Mehlich) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lw%C3%B3w_-_Widok_z_wie%C5%BCy_ratuszowej_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lviv lies on the edge of the Roztochia Upland, about 70 km east of the Polish border and 160 km north of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a>. Its highest point is the Vysokyi Zamok, or High Castle hill, which rises 409 m above sea level and overlooks the historic centre with its green-domed churches. The city has been known by many names, among them Lwów in Polish, Lemberg in German, Lvov in Russian and Leopolis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 14th century Lviv became the heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-ruthenia">Red Ruthenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, overtaking older cities such as Halych, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelm">Chełm</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belz">Belz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl">Przemyśl</a>. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-galicia-volhynia">Kingdom of Galicia–Volhynia</a> from 1272 to 1340. In the wars over the succession King Casimir III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> captured the city in 1340 and burned the old princely castle, and Poland gained control of Lviv and its region in 1349. On 17 June 1356 Casimir moved the city to a new site and granted it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> rights, under which a council elected by the wealthy citizens decided city matters. In 1358 it became the seat of a Roman Catholic archdiocese.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy annexed the region, and Lemberg became the capital of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodomeria">Lodomeria</a>. Its population grew from about 30,000 in 1772 to 196,000 by 1910, with urban poverty alongside the growth. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the city became a battleground between the Polish population and the Ukrainian Sich Riflemen, and on the night of 31 October to 1 November 1918 the Western Ukrainian People's Republic was proclaimed with Lviv as capital. In the interwar Second Polish Republic Lwów was, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, the second cultural and academic centre, home to the Lwów School of Mathematics.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> entered Lwów on 22 September 1939, and the city became the capital of the new Lviv Oblast. After the war it remained in the Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic, and most of the remaining Polish population was expelled to the former German territories, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>. Since 1991 Lviv has been part of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lviv is the largest city of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and the administrative centre of Lviv Oblast. It has Lviv University and Lviv Polytechnic. Its historic heart has cobblestone streets and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>, Neoclassicist and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Art Nouveau</a> architecture, and is listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lviv” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Franz Conrad von Hötzendorf's offensive planning was badly at odds with the army's real capabilities: in 1914 the invasions of Serbia were repulsed at Cer and the Kolubara, while in Galicia the army was routed around <b>Lemberg</b> (Lviv), losing much of its trained peacetime officer corps within months.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His successors, Lațcu and Petru I Mușat, built the institutions of a state: Lațcu sought a Catholic bishopric at Siret around 1371, while Petru I struck coins, did homage to Władysław II Jagiełło of Poland at <b>Lviv</b> in 1387 to balance Hungary, and established the court at Suceava.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…a series of wars fought by the new Polish Army from 1918 to 1921: the Polish–Ukrainian War from <b>Lwów</b>, the Greater Poland uprising, the Polish–Czechoslovak War over Trans-Olza, the Polish–Lithuanian War, three Silesian Uprisings and, most critical, the Polish–Soviet War (1919–1921), which ended in a decisive Polish victory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 21 January 1990 hundreds of thousands formed a human chain from Kyiv to <b>Lviv</b> and Ivano-Frankivsk.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The poem was first published in 1863 in the fourth issue of the <b>Lviv</b> journal Meta, which wrongly attributed it to Taras Shevchenko.</blockquote><a href="/songs/national-anthem-of-ukraine">State Anthem of Ukraine</a></li><li><blockquote>The song became popular through Stepan Charnetskyi's stage performance Sun of the Ruin, based on a play by Vasyl Pachovsky, which premiered in early 1914 with the melody arranged by the conductor Mykhailo Kossak and was played in <b>Lviv</b>'s Ruska Besida theatre and across Eastern Galicia.</blockquote><a href="/songs/oi-u-luzi-chervona-kalyna">Oh, the Red Viburnum in the Meadow</a></li></ul></section>
